When declarative attributes (`required`, `pattern`) can't express a rule — "password and confirm-password must match," for instance — the Constraint Validation API lets you plug custom logic into the same native validation pipeline, rather than bypassing it with a fully custom JS-only check:

```js
const confirmField = form.querySelector('#confirm-password');

function checkMatch() {
  if (confirmField.value !== passwordField.value) {
    confirmField.setCustomValidity('Passwords must match');
  } else {
    confirmField.setCustomValidity(''); // clears the error — required to pass validation again
  }
}

confirmField.addEventListener('input', checkMatch);
```

`setCustomValidity()` marks the field invalid with your own message, and the browser treats it exactly like a native validation failure — it blocks submission and shows your message in the same native tooltip UI. The gotcha everyone hits once: forgetting to call `setCustomValidity('')` when the condition becomes valid again permanently locks the field as invalid, since the browser has no way to know the custom error has been resolved unless you tell it.
